Event-driven architecture is an architectural pattern where services communicate through events
representing state changes or business actions. Producers publish events to a broker, 
and consumers independently process those events, enabling loose coupling and asynchronous communication.


In simple terms:
Event: A record of something that happened (e.g., “OrderPlaced”, “UserSignedUp”).

Event producer: The part of the system that emits the event.
Event consumer: The part of the system that listens for and reacts to the event.



Traditional synchronous architecture
Order Service
     |
     | HTTP call
     v
Payment Service
     |
     | HTTP call
     v
Notification Service

Order Service has to know about Payment Service and Notification Service.
If Notification Service is down, it can potentially affect the flow.



Real-world example

Suppose a customer places an order:

1. Order Service
       |
       | Publish OrderCreated
       v
2. Kafka
       |
       +------> Inventory Service
       |
       +------> Payment Service
       |
       +------> Notification Service
       |
       +------> Analytics Service





Loose coupling → services don't depend directly on each other
Scalability → consumers can scale independently
Asynchronous processing → producer doesn't wait for every consumer
Extensibility → adding a new consumer doesn't require changing the producer
Resilience → events can be retained/retried depending on the messaging system



One important distinction

Event-driven architecture ≠ Kafka

EDA is the architectural pattern.
Kafka is one technology you can use to implement it.


Other options include:

Kafka
AWS SQS/SNS
RabbitMQ
Google Pub/Sub
Azure Service Bus
